# KNOWN GAP — the `Box.new` HALF of the field-source double free.
#
# This is `tests/fixtures/box_ctor_field_source_owning_string.gg` with exactly
# one token changed: `Box[String](w1.s)` becomes `Box.new(w1.s)`. The bare-ctor
# spelling now clones the still-live field and exits 0; this spelling still
# takes a shallow copy and dies with
#
#     free(): double free detected in tcache 2
#
# on both backends, with `gg check` clean.
#
# ⚠ IT IS THE SAME OPERATION AND THE SPELLING IS THE ONE THE BOOK TEACHES
# (`docs/book/16-smart-pointers.md`). The two spellings are lowered by two
# hand-written mints that have drifted apart on four axes — which mint you get,
# `call` vs `call_extern`, drop registration, and shim ordering — under a
# comment asserting they produce identical IR. The self-host already has the
# reference-grade shape: it normalises `Box.new` into the one ctor path.
#
# THE FIX is to unify the two mints so `Box.new(x)` IS `Box(x)`, not to
# hand-copy the ctor's ownership fix into the second mint.
#
# ⛔ HEAP-FORCE — see the sibling fixture's header: a string LITERAL is a
# `.rodata` view with `cap = 0` and exits 0 even under the defect.
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT (what the language SHOULD do): built
# EXPECTED EXIT: 0, and clean under ASan.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

struct W:
    String s

int main():
    W w1 = W(mk("aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa", "bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb"))
    Box[String] b = Box.new(w1.s)
    print("built")
    return 0
